1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Right now, the phone in your pocket

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,800
can work out where you are two within a few meters.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,040
And here is the strange part.

4
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,840
It never sends a single message to space.

5
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,520
It only listens.

6
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,680
So how can a device that never talks to a satellite

7
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:16,960
know exactly where it is standing?

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:20,200
The answer is a story about clocks, distance,

9
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,760
and a correction that comes straight from Einstein.

10
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:27,200
GPS is a group of about 30 satellites

11
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,120
run by the United States.

12
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:33,440
They circle the Earth at roughly 20,200 kilometers up,

13
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:36,560
far higher than the International Space Station.

14
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:39,880
Each one goes around the planet about twice a day.

15
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:43,480
They are spread out so that, from almost anywhere on Earth,

16
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:46,840
at least four of them are above the horizon at any moment.

17
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:52,320
Every satellite broadcasts a simple message over and over.

18
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:54,000
It says two things.

19
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,880
Here is exactly where I am in my orbit.

20
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:58,960
And here is exactly what time it was

21
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,320
when I sent this.

22
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:01,760
That is all.

23
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:04,320
The satellite does not know you exist.

24
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:08,040
Your phone simply catches these messages as they arrive.

25
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:11,400
Now comes the clever part.

26
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,880
The signal travels at the speed of light,

27
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:17,240
about 300,000 kilometers every second.

28
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,400
If your phone knows when the message left the satellite,

29
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:23,960
and when it arrived, it can multiply that travel time

30
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:25,440
by the speed of light.

31
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:28,400
The result is the distance to that satellite.

32
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:32,200
For GPS, that trip takes less than a tenth of a second.

33
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:37,120
Knowing your distance from one satellite is not enough.

34
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:40,440
It only tells you that you are somewhere on a giant sphere

35
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:42,080
around that satellite.

36
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:44,880
Picture it as a circle on a flat map.

37
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:46,720
You could be anywhere on that line.

38
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:51,480
Add a second satellite and you get a second circle.

39
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:54,520
The two circles cross in only two places.

40
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:58,520
Add a third and only one point sits on all three circles.

41
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:00,040
That point is you.

42
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:02,880
This method is called triliteration.

43
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:04,880
And it works because each distance

44
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:07,720
removes a huge number of possible positions.

45
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:10,840
But there is a problem.

46
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:12,720
All of this depends on time,

47
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:15,000
and the clock in your phone is cheap.

48
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,280
Light travels 300 meters in a single microsecond.

49
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:23,520
So if your phone's clock is off by just 1,000th of a second,

50
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:28,320
every distance it calculates is wrong by about 300 kilometers.

51
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:30,080
That would put you in the wrong city.

52
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:33,520
The solution is elegant.

53
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:35,560
Instead of trusting its own clock,

54
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:39,040
the phone treats the clock error as one more unknown.

55
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,080
Its position has three unknowns, east, west,

56
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:43,920
north, south, and height.

57
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:46,400
Add the clock error and there are four.

58
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:49,120
Four unknowns need four measurements.

59
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,920
So the phone listens to at least four satellites

60
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,960
and solves for all of them at once.

61
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:58,560
That is why the satellites carry atomic clocks.

62
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:01,280
These clocks count the vibrations of atoms,

63
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:04,080
which happen at an extremely steady rate.

64
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:06,440
Because the whole system depends on them,

65
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:09,280
GPS has quietly become one of the world's

66
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:11,600
most important time sources.

67
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:14,000
Phone networks, power grids and banks

68
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,520
all use GPS time to stay in sync.

69
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:20,360
And here is where Einstein comes in.

70
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:22,640
Because the satellites move fast,

71
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:27,320
their clocks run slightly slow by about seven microseconds a day.

72
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:30,000
But because they are farther from Earth's gravity,

73
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,000
their clocks also run fast by about 45 microseconds a day.

74
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,080
Together, the satellite clocks gain

75
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:39,760
about 38 microseconds every day.

76
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:43,120
If engineers ignored this, GPS positions

77
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:46,040
would drift by roughly 10 kilometers a day.

78
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:49,360
So the clocks are adjusted to account for relativity.

79
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:54,040
Even with all of this, GPS is not perfect.

80
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:56,200
The signal slows down slightly

81
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,680
as it passes through the upper atmosphere.

82
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:02,640
In cities, it bounces off tall buildings before reaching you.

83
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:05,040
And if the visible satellites are bunched together

84
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:08,600
in one part of the sky, small errors get bigger.

85
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:10,280
This is why your location,

86
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:13,400
sometimes jumps around between skyscrapers.

87
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:16,880
So every time you open a map,

88
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:18,640
your phone is quietly listening

89
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:21,720
to satellites 20,000 kilometers away,

90
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:23,240
timing their signals,

91
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,200
turning time into distance,

92
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:26,880
solving four equations,

93
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:29,000
and correcting for Einstein.

94
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
All of that happens in a moment,

95
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,680
and it never says a word back.
